---
const monitors = [
  { name: "Storefront", ok: true, bars: "gggggggggggggggggggg" },
  { name: "Checkout API", ok: true, bars: "gggggggggrgggggggggg" },
  { name: "Health", ok: false, bars: "gggggggggggggggrrrrr" },
];
---

<section id="uptime" class="border-y border-line bg-surface px-10 py-16 max-md:px-5 max-md:py-12">
  <div
    class="container-site grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.1fr)_minmax(0,0.9fr)] items-center gap-12 max-lg:grid-cols-1 max-lg:gap-8"
  >
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3.5">
      <span class="eyebrow">Uptime</span>
      <h2 class="m-0 text-[32px] font-semibold leading-[1.18] tracking-[-0.025em] [text-wrap:balance] max-md:text-[26px]">
        The site being down is the easy case.
      </h2>
      <p class="m-0 max-w-[520px] text-[15.5px] leading-[1.6] text-body [text-wrap:pretty]">
        Point Zenguy at an endpoint and it checks every five minutes. Unlimited monitors, never
        counted as a run. The browser is for the flow that can fail while everything still says 200.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 rounded-[14px] border border-line bg-cream px-5 py-4">
      {monitors.map((m) => (
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <span
            class:list={[
              "inline-flex w-[52px] shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium",
              m.ok ? "text-green" : "text-red",
            ]}
          >
            <span class:list={["h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full", m.ok ? "bg-green" : "bg-red"]}></span>
            {m.ok ? "Up" : "Down"}
          </span>
          <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-[13.5px] font-medium">{m.name}</span>
          <div class="flex h-3.5 w-[120px] shrink-0 items-stretch gap-0.5" role="img" aria-label={`Last checks for ${m.name}`}>
            {m.bars.split("").map((b) => (
              <span class:list={["flex-1 rounded-[1px]", b === "g" ? "bg-green" : "bg-red"]}></span>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
